Article 7
(1) Regional Government conducts prevention as referred to in article 6 letter a by means of: a. socializing laws and regulations- b. providing counseling/guidance; c. providing education on the dangers of domestic violence; d. conducting seminars/workshops or the like; e. forming work networks in efforts to prevent violence; f. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns; g. forming a violence prevention system, mapping locations or areas prone to violence; and h. increasing public awareness in behaving in accordance with religious norms and customs (2) In addition to the Regional Government, prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) must be implemented by:
a. Religious institutions;
b. family and/or closest relatives;
c. customary leaders;
d. customary institutions;
e. community;
f. educational institutions;
g. health institutions;
h. social community institutions; and i. business sector and other institutions.

Article 22
(1) The Regent coordinates the protection of women and children from acts of violence with: a. the government;
b. other Regency/regional governments;
c. vertical agencies;
d. educational institutions; and
e. community institutions.
(2) Coordination as referred to in paragraph (1) includes: a. religious affairs;
b. health affairs;
b. education affairs;
c. social affairs;
d. legal affairs;
e. labor affairs; and f. other affairs.

CHAPTER II
PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES
Article 2
The Protection of Women and Children from Acts of Violence, is implemented based on the principles of:
a. respect and fulfillment of the rights of Victims of Acts of Violence;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. the best interests of Victims of acts of violence; and
e. The principle of the right to life, survival and development,

Article 13
(1) The provisions regarding prevention and services for acts of violence protection of women as referred to in Article 7 up to Article 12 apply mutatis mutandis to the protection of children from acts of violence;
(2) In addition to carrying out prevention as referred to in paragraph (1), also by means of increasing family capacity in child protection and child care skills.

Article 12
(1) The protection of children who are victims of acts of violence as referred to in article 5 letter a shall be the responsibility of the Regional Government. (2) The protection of child victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by means of: a. formulating policies for prevention, reduction of vulnerable risks, handling of victims and child data and information systems; b. providing maintenance, care, and social rehabilitation for neglected children, both within institutions and outside institutions;
c. providing shelters, maintenance and care for neglected children; and d. providing special protection to children.

